52. INTERVIEW QUESTIONS — BASIC
Q1. What is JavaScript?
Answer
JavaScript is a dynamically typed programming language used to add behaviour and interactivity to webpages. It runs in browsers and can also run outside browsers using environments such as Node.js.

Q2. What is the difference between HTML, CSS and JavaScript?
Answer
HTML provides structure, CSS controls presentation, and JavaScript controls behaviour and interactivity.

Q3. What is a JavaScript engine?
Answer
A JavaScript engine is software that parses and executes JavaScript code. For example, Chrome uses V8, Firefox uses SpiderMonkey, and Safari uses JavaScriptCore.

Q4. What is console.log()?
Answer
console.log() prints values to the developer console. It is commonly used for learning, debugging and inspecting program behaviour.

Q5. What is ECMAScript?
Answer
ECMAScript is the standardized specification that defines the JavaScript language.



53. INTERMEDIATE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
Q6. Is JavaScript compiled or interpreted?
Answer
JavaScript is traditionally described as an interpreted language, but modern JavaScript engines use just-in-time compilation to improve execution performance.

Q7. Are semicolons mandatory?
Answer
No. JavaScript has Automatic Semicolon Insertion, so semicolons can often be omitted. However, using them consistently can improve readability and avoid certain edge cases.

Q8. What is the difference between syntax and runtime errors?
Answer
A syntax error occurs when the JavaScript code cannot be correctly parsed. A runtime error occurs after execution has started and something fails during execution.

Q9. Why does this fail?
console.log(Hello);
Answer
Hello is interpreted as an identifier rather than text.
If no variable called Hello exists, JavaScript throws a ReferenceError.
Correct:
console.log("Hello");

54. SCENARIO-BASED INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
Scenario 1
The console says ReferenceError: total is not defined. What do you check?
Answer:
I would first locate the line causing the error, check whether total was declared, and verify its spelling and scope. Since it is a runtime error, statements before that line may already have executed.

Scenario 2
Nothing is printed and there is no error. What would you check?
Answer:
I would check whether the code is inside an unclosed comment or string, whether the JavaScript file is correctly linked, whether the file was saved, and whether the browser console is filtering the messages.

Scenario 3
Why is this different?
console.log("Hello", "World");
vs
console.log("Hello" + "World");
Answer:
The comma passes two separate arguments to console.log, which displays them with a space. The + operator joins the two strings first, producing HelloWorld.

